# Intuition
The problem heavily distinguishes between the *first* time an item is purchased and any *subsequent* purchases. Buying an item for the first time triggers a "free bonus" of all items whose factors are multiples of the purchased item's factor. Subsequent purchases of the same item yield exactly 1 item and no extra bonuses. 

This maps perfectly to a combination of two standard algorithms:
1. **0/1 Knapsack:** To maximize the unique "first purchase" bonuses.
2. **Greedy:** To maximize the raw quantity of items bought with whatever budget is left over (which is simply buying as many copies of the absolute cheapest item as possible).

# Approach
1. **Factor Frequency & Minimum Price:** First, iterate through the items to find the `min_price` (for the greedy step later) and record the frequency of each factor in a static array.
2. **Harmonic Precomputation ($O(F \log F)$):** Instead of an $O(N^2)$ loop to see which items divide which, we use the factor frequency array. For each factor `f`, we iterate through its multiples (`f`, `2f`, `3f`, ...) up to the maximum factor. The sum of the frequencies of these multiples represents the exact number of items we get when buying `f` for the first time.
3. **0/1 Knapsack DP:** We initialize a DP array where `dp[b]` represents the maximum items we can collect by spending exactly `b` budget on "first purchases". For each item, we update the DP array backwards to ensure we only "buy" the first-purchase bonus once per item. We bound the loop by dynamically tracking the maximum reachable budget (`max_b`) to skip useless zero-filled spaces.
4. **Greedy Fill:** Finally, we evaluate every reachable budget state `b` in the DP array. We take the items gained from the knapsack (`dp[b]`) and greedily spend the remaining `budget - b` on the absolute cheapest item. We return the maximum total items found.

# Complexity
- Time complexity:
$$\mathcal{O}(N \cdot B + F \log F)$$
Where $N$ is the number of items, $B$ is the `budget`, and $F$ is the maximum factor. The precomputation step takes $\mathcal{O}(F \log F)$ using a harmonic series. The Knapsack DP takes $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot B)$. Given $N, B, F \le 1500$, the operations are well under $10^7$, ensuring a 0ms runtime.

- Space complexity:
$$\mathcal{O}(B + F)$$
We strictly use fixed-size 1D primitive arrays of size 1505 for the budget states and factor counts. This avoids heap allocations entirely, resulting in $\mathcal{O}(1)$ practical overhead and excellent cache locality.

# Code
```cpp []
#pragma GCC optimize("O3,unroll-loops")
#pragma GCC target("avx2,bmi,bmi2,lzcnt,popcnt")

#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// Fast I/O to strip standard streaming overhead
static const auto __io_speed_up = []() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    return 0;
}();

class Solution {
public:
    int maximumSaleItems(vector<vector<int>>& items, int budget) {
        int max_factor = 0;
        int min_price = 2000; // Constraints state budget & price <= 1500
        
        int factor_counts[1505] = {0};
        
        // 1. Single pass to count factors, and find absolute bounds
        for (const auto& item : items) {
            int f = item[0];
            int p = item[1];
            factor_counts[f]++;
            if (f > max_factor) max_factor = f;
            if (p < min_price) min_price = p;
        }
        
        // 2. O(F log F) Precomputation for the "First Purchase Bonus"
        // factor_value[f] = exactly how many total items you obtain when you buy factor f
        int factor_value[1505] = {0};
        for (int f = 1; f <= max_factor; ++f) {
            if (factor_counts[f] > 0) {
                int v = 0;
                // Sum frequencies of all multiples of f
                for (int mult = f; mult <= max_factor; mult += f) {
                    v += factor_counts[mult];
                }
                factor_value[f] = v;
            }
        }
        
        // 3. 0/1 Knapsack using ultra-fast fixed array
        int dp[1505];
        for (int i = 1; i <= budget; ++i) {
            dp[i] = -1;
        }
        dp[0] = 0;
        
        int max_b = 0; // Tracks dynamically reached DP limits to cut iteration sizes
        
        for (const auto& item : items) {
            int p = item[1];
            if (p > budget) continue;
            
            int v = factor_value[item[0]];
            int current_max = budget < (max_b + p) ? budget : (max_b + p);
            
            for (int b = current_max; b >= p; --b) {
                if (dp[b - p] != -1) {
                    int new_val = dp[b - p] + v;
                    if (new_val > dp[b]) {
                        dp[b] = new_val;
                    }
                }
            }
            max_b = current_max;
        }
        
        // 4. Extract combination of DP + Greedily buying cheapest extra items
        int max_items = 0;
        for (int b = 0; b <= budget; ++b) {
            if (dp[b] != -1) {
                int current_items = dp[b] + (budget - b) / min_price;
                if (current_items > max_items) {
                    max_items = current_items;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return max_items;
    }
};
